  "slug": "find-the-length-of-the-longest-common-prefix",
  "title": "Find the Length of the Longest Common Prefix",
  "topic": "arrays-hashing",
  "difficulty": "Medium",
  "pattern": "incremental prefix hashing",
  "statement": "Every integer in `nums` is at least `1`, so its decimal form has no leading zeros. For two different indices `i` and `j`, the common prefix length is the largest `L` such that the first `L` digits of `nums[i]` equal the first `L` digits of `nums[j]`.\n\nReturn the largest common prefix length over all pairs of different indices. If no two numbers share their first digit, return `0`.\n\nConstraints: `1 <= nums.length <= 200` and `1 <= nums[i] <= 10000`.\n",
  "examples": [
    {
      "input": "nums = [1]",
      "output": "0",
      "explanation": "Produced by the reference solution, verified against all four implementations."
    },
    {
      "input": "nums = [4890, 8318, 9978, 3259]",
      "output": "0",
      "explanation": "Produced by the reference solution, verified against all four implementations."
    }
  ],
  "constraints": [
    "Function signature: `function solve(nums: number[])`.",
    "Inputs are JSON-serializable values satisfying the bounds stated above."
  ],
  "hints": [
    "Two numbers share a prefix of length L exactly when the same L-character string starts both of them; ask yourself which strings you would have to remember to detect that.",
    "For each number, walk its digits and record every prefix length 1, 2, 3, ... as you go.",
    "You never need the pair itself, only its length, so keep a running maximum over the prefixes that were already recorded before you added them."
  ],
  "editorial": {
    "approach": "The brute force turns every number into its digit string and compares all O(n\u00b2) pairs character by character up to the shorter length, keeping the longest match. The optimal pass uses the equivalence 'a pair shares a prefix of length L exactly when the same L-digit string is a prefix of two different numbers': walk each number left to right, emitting its prefixes of length 1, 2, 3, ..., and drop each prefix into a hash set. The moment a prefix is already in the set, two different numbers share it, so length L is achievable, and the largest L that ever hits is the answer. The work is one set operation per digit, O(n * d) with d <= 5.",
    "why_optimal": "Each digit of each number has to be read at least once, so \u03a9(n * d) is the floor and the prefix set sits exactly on it; nothing cheaper can decide whether some pair agrees on 5 digits. The brute force is O(n\u00b2 * d), which is 200x more comparisons at n = 200, and the gap widens with n. The d factor is capped at 5 because nums[i] <= 10000, so the optimal pass is effectively linear in the array length.",
    "pitfalls": "Comparing digits without clamping to the shorter string reads past its end. A single-element array must return 0, and it will only do that if best starts at 0 rather than at the digit count of the lone element. Because values are >= 1 there is no zero and no leading-zero string, so no empty-prefix special case is needed. The self-match trap: if you check a number's own prefixes against a set you have already updated with them, one number can appear to share a prefix with itself, which would report its full digit count; checking before inserting is safe only because two prefixes of the same number are never the same string, so keep the check-then-insert order."
  },
